    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d635">

                <pb facs="0755" n="749" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg749"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/1197: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Henderson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-12-10T00:00:00+03:00">December 10,
                        1942—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received December 12—1:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1079. For Ambassador Standley. Before leaving Washington, you may desire to
                    acquaint yourself by an examination of reports in the Navy Department with
                    regard to the unsatisfactory relations which appear to be developing between
                    Americans and British in north Russia. It is my understanding that
                        Roullard<anchor n="69" xml:id="d635-f1"/> has reported confidentially that
                    one of the chief causes of these differences is British insistence upon loading
                    American ships with British cargoes with the result that the Russians are unable
                    to fulfill commitments to us. Thompson tells me that Faymonville, in discussing
                    the matter personally with him appears to feel that the whole general question
                    of the setup in north Russia, particularly the relationship between the British
                    and Americans, should be reconsidered.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henderson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d635-f1" n="69" xml:id="d635-f1-content">
                <p>Lt. Comdr. George D. Roullard, Assistant Naval Attaché and Assistant Naval
                    Attaché for Air in the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
